WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.000
There we are. 

00:04.000 --> 00:10.000
The people we came from go right back

00:12.000 --> 00:18.000
to the time when they believed that the God…

00:20.000 --> 00:24.000
the bounty was actually the elements.

00:25.000 --> 00:33.000
And the… deferring to the elements has followed our people

00:33.000 --> 00:37.000
right back through Druid times, through Pictish times.

00:38.000 --> 00:44.000
That you believed that, first of all, there is a…

00:44.000 --> 00:50.000
they always believed that there was a being who made the rhythm,

00:51.000 --> 00:59.000
the rhythm of life, which, if you will sit on the shore you will realise,

00:59.000 --> 01:02.000
is actually music, it’s like a symphony,

01:02.000 --> 01:05.000
it’s like a piece of music. Everything fits in.

01:05.000 --> 01:10.000
You have the main things happening: the spring, summer, autumn, winter.

01:11.000 --> 01:15.000
You have the spring tides and the neap tides. 

01:16.000 --> 01:19.000
You have the morning and the night.

01:21.000 --> 01:23.000
And everything fits into that.

01:24.000 --> 01:27.000
Now this superior being, first of all,

01:27.000 --> 01:33.000
created these islands in the west with a rim of gold round them,

01:33.000 --> 01:38.000
which is the richness of the fish, the shellfish and so on.

01:38.000 --> 01:42.000
In very early times, they lived mostly off shellfish,

01:42.000 --> 01:46.000
they had not yet learned how to use nets properly,

01:46.000 --> 01:49.000
but when they did, it was a basic sort of net 

01:49.000 --> 01:53.000
in which they would catch, not only fish, but seabirds –

01:53.000 --> 01:55.000
anything that was near the shore. 

01:56.000 --> 01:59.000
As succeeding waves of people came,

01:59.000 --> 02:02.000
the original people were small people,

02:03.000 --> 02:06.000
and they eventually, the little people, the small people 

02:06.000 --> 02:10.000
were pushed further and further off the agricultural land,

02:10.000 --> 02:12.000
right down to the shore. 

02:12.000 --> 02:16.000
And they would eat and come out just at night.

02:16.000 --> 02:20.000
They would go and pick shellfish.

02:21.000 --> 02:29.000
They would have basic herbs like silverweed, wild thyme,

02:31.000 --> 02:37.000
all these native herbs, and they had, they would pound them in a… 

02:37.000 --> 02:41.000
with a mortar and pestle stone, of course,

02:41.000 --> 02:46.000
and they would dry all the seeds and all the roots,

02:46.000 --> 02:49.000
and they would mix that with the shellfish.

02:49.000 --> 02:53.000
And history has proved that they had the 

02:53.000 --> 02:55.000
most balanced diet in the world.

02:55.000 --> 02:57.000
They were small, they were wiry and 

02:57.000 --> 02:59.000
they were strong people and they were happy people

02:59.000 --> 03:02.000
because their God never let them down.

03:03.000 --> 03:06.000
The elements looked after their lives.

03:07.000 --> 03:12.000
The wind came at the right time to ripen the food.

03:13.000 --> 03:19.000
The spring came when the… life was brought alive again 

03:19.000 --> 03:22.000
after the restful winter of nature,

03:22.000 --> 03:27.000
and then it went on through the spring into a summer into the winter.

03:27.000 --> 03:29.000
And the winter was the nicest time of all,

03:29.000 --> 03:32.000
because they would generally live underground

03:32.000 --> 03:38.000
and light and dark did not matter all that much.

03:39.000 --> 03:44.000
But the winter was a celebrating time when you ate and drank 

03:45.000 --> 03:50.000
everything you had preserved from the harvest.

03:50.000 --> 03:53.000
And so you had a marvellous little people. 

03:53.000 --> 03:55.000
There were waves and waves of people

03:55.000 --> 03:58.000
and eventually people of normal size arrived.

03:58.000 --> 04:01.000
These little people lived underground

04:01.000 --> 04:04.000
and they had very basic needs. 

04:05.000 --> 04:13.000
They would have skins to lie on, they would have skins to wear,

04:14.000 --> 04:16.000
pelts of various kinds.

04:16.000 --> 04:24.000
And they were a musical people, and also they were,

04:24.000 --> 04:30.000
a very intelligent people who knew how to harness nature. 

04:31.000 --> 04:36.000
So, that was the sort of background of these Hebrides,

04:36.000 --> 04:38.000
that was the sorts of things that were happening. 

04:38.000 --> 04:45.000
From all that, later things would reveal

04:45.000 --> 04:52.000
that there were people, strange little people

04:52.000 --> 04:57.000
living underground and they would hear this beautiful music,

04:57.000 --> 04:59.000
especially on a moonlit night.

04:59.000 --> 05:02.000
So the next wave of people coming in would wander off

05:02.000 --> 05:03.000
to the machair, to the shore 

05:03.000 --> 05:06.000
and they would come to the sand dunes which was…

05:06.000 --> 05:10.000
and they would hear the music coming out of these places.

05:10.000 --> 05:15.000
And they would probe sometimes deeper 

05:15.000 --> 05:18.000
and they would find the entry and go in and look.

05:19.000 --> 05:27.000
And they would be entertained by the happy little people

05:27.000 --> 05:30.000
who lived, close to nature, underground.

05:30.000 --> 05:34.000
And there would be times when they would be

05:34.000 --> 05:36.000
a bit bewitched by the drink.

05:36.000 --> 05:38.000
The little people, you see, would come out during the night

05:38.000 --> 05:44.000
and take the ears of corn and they would ferment them

05:44.000 --> 05:47.000
and turn them into a fine alcohol.

05:48.000 --> 05:51.000
They would take anything, they would milk the cows at night

05:51.000 --> 05:55.000
and sometimes the lady or, would come out to milk the cow in the morning and

05:55.000 --> 05:57.000
find the cow was bewitched.

05:57.000 --> 06:00.000
Not at all, it had been milked during the night.

06:01.000 --> 06:04.000
Then there was the stories of the changeling.

06:04.000 --> 06:09.000
Easily… easily understood when they would be at the harvest

06:09.000 --> 06:14.000
on the machair, and they would leave the baby under…

06:16.000 --> 06:19.000
sheafs of corn and come back later to find a

06:19.000 --> 06:24.000
little, dirty, old man there where they had put their baby.

06:24.000 --> 06:29.000
And so it’s possible that these things all took place in history. 

06:29.000 --> 06:33.000
That these little people would have put one of their old people

06:33.000 --> 06:37.000
who were useless and stolen that nice little baby in its place.

06:37.000 --> 06:40.000
Believe it or not, it’s not important.

06:41.000 --> 06:46.000
So generally you have then all this mystery about it, 

06:46.000 --> 06:48.000
and recent times of course have proved,

06:48.000 --> 06:52.000
go to Lewis, go anywhere and you’ll find these wheel-houses

06:52.000 --> 06:56.000
and combinations of many wheel-houses, wheel-house villages,

06:56.000 --> 06:59.000
of various kinds, they’re everywhere throughout the Hebrides. 

07:00.000 --> 07:04.000
Also, during that particular period were the souterrain,

07:04.000 --> 07:07.000
the other kind of souterrain where there were…

07:07.000 --> 07:11.000
We discovered ones here just a few years ago.

07:11.000 --> 07:15.000
And there were many stories about them and

07:15.000 --> 07:19.000
they would only be about a foot deep, 

07:19.000 --> 07:21.000
about a foot and a half wide,

07:21.000 --> 07:26.000
and they would go back several, several yards underground.

07:26.000 --> 07:29.000
Many theories, the nicest one I heard was that 

07:29.000 --> 07:32.000
during the slave trade in Athens and Rome,

07:32.000 --> 07:39.000
remember we had very shallow seas round us. 

07:39.000 --> 07:45.000
During that slave trade that… the Mediterranean type of boat,

07:45.000 --> 07:50.000
which would be a house on a very broad wooden base,

07:50.000 --> 07:53.000
and there would have one big lug sail,

07:53.000 --> 07:55.000
and there would be no hurry.

07:55.000 --> 07:57.000
But they could come right close to shore

07:57.000 --> 08:03.000
and we do know that they did come and steal

08:03.000 --> 08:08.000
young, strong, young people and sell them in Athens and Rome.

08:08.000 --> 08:12.000
And that, when the locals would get used to the idea,

08:12.000 --> 08:15.000
that they would say to themselves, well,

08:15.000 --> 08:18.000
if they saw the sail coming, the enemy sail, 

08:18.000 --> 08:20.000
they would put their wives and children in,

08:20.000 --> 08:25.000
crawling in backwards, and the man of the house would be,

08:25.000 --> 08:29.000
sort of, above ground keeping any eye on everything.

08:29.000 --> 08:31.000
They could live in these souterrains,

08:31.000 --> 08:34.000
which were just tunnels,

08:34.000 --> 08:38.000
they could live in them for days, as long as it took.

08:38.000 --> 08:43.000
So they had that basic sort of intelligent approach

08:43.000 --> 08:48.000
to the big world outside, but they were happy. 

08:51.000 --> 08:57.000
But later, Christianity arrived and God revealed himself

08:57.000 --> 09:02.000
to the special people, to Jews and until then

09:02.000 --> 09:04.000
nobody had known what God was like.

09:04.000 --> 09:07.000
Then suddenly, the Jews knew.

09:07.000 --> 09:09.000
But the Jews thought it was only for them,

09:09.000 --> 09:14.000
that nobody else was never to know or get to know this God.

09:14.000 --> 09:21.000
And then there’s various stories of how Creation happened,

09:21.000 --> 09:28.000
how the fall of man happened and how Redemption happened.

09:28.000 --> 09:33.000
And then the good news of the gospel was brought to these shores.

09:34.000 --> 09:38.000
Well we’re now just going back the best part of two millenia.

09:39.000 --> 09:41.000
Why? Because you see the,

09:41.000 --> 09:46.000
when you consider that the shores near Palestine,

09:47.000 --> 09:50.000
take Yugoslavia as an example, 

09:51.000 --> 09:56.000
they never heard about Jesus Christ until the 11th century,

09:56.000 --> 09:58.000
until St. Cyril went there.

09:58.000 --> 10:00.000
Now that’s extraordinary!

10:00.000 --> 10:05.000
Because here, we already knew by before the year 400,

10:05.000 --> 10:08.000
about him because of the Roman invasion,

10:08.000 --> 10:11.000
the occupation of lower Britain.

10:11.000 --> 10:17.000
We knew already the story of Creation and Redemption 

10:17.000 --> 10:20.000
had arrived on these shores as early as that.

10:20.000 --> 10:24.000
So we were fortunate, in a way,

10:24.000 --> 10:28.000
that the great Roman occupation had taken place,

10:28.000 --> 10:31.000
so we were well-versed in these things.

10:31.000 --> 10:34.000
Also, there was a tremendous movement of people,

10:34.000 --> 10:39.000
remember the historical side of our people was that they 

10:39.000 --> 10:42.000
would have started off somewhere about India,

10:42.000 --> 10:45.000
certainly in the far East, and they would have taken

10:45.000 --> 10:48.000
centuries and centuries and centuries to get across here.

10:48.000 --> 10:54.000
And if you take from the… from say the lower parts there,

10:55.000 --> 10:59.000
take Albania or Yugoslavia, and look at the faces of the women, 

10:59.000 --> 11:04.000
all the way up until you reach the Russian steps, and the Baltic,

11:04.000 --> 11:07.000
and you’ll see that they’ve got the same features

11:07.000 --> 11:10.000
as the old Highland, island women.

11:10.000 --> 11:15.000
Even, you know, physically they were similar, very similar. 

11:15.000 --> 11:20.000
Strong, well-built, broad-backed women.

11:20.000 --> 11:25.000
The men were not important, it was the women, because the traits,

11:25.000 --> 11:29.000
the physical traits would come through the mothers so…

11:29.000 --> 11:32.000
a good wife was of the essence in those days. 

11:32.000 --> 11:37.000
They would have spent centuries,

11:37.000 --> 11:40.000
many generations in each place before they moved on and

11:40.000 --> 11:45.000
until they eventually reached up the west of Spain

11:45.000 --> 11:47.000
and right up the French coast,

11:47.000 --> 11:52.000
and then over to the Isle of Man, Ireland and well Cornwall, Wales,

11:52.000 --> 11:57.000
and the Scottish islands – the seven Celtic countries. 

11:58.000 --> 12:00.000
Now, they brought with them their own particular

12:00.000 --> 12:04.000
kind of culture, particularly their own music, 

12:04.000 --> 12:08.000
whereas the strange music of the English ears was 

12:08.000 --> 12:11.000
very, very strange, very limited.

12:12.000 --> 12:15.000
Their music, of course, was the pentatonic scale,

12:15.000 --> 12:20.000
with grace notes which exist and survive to this day, the grace notes. 

12:20.000 --> 12:26.000
And that’s why they made such a terrible mess of everything

12:27.000 --> 12:30.000
when they came towards the end of the last century,

12:30.000 --> 12:35.000
when it became the darling thing of the drawing rooms 

12:35.000 --> 12:38.000
in Edinburgh and London further afield, 

12:38.000 --> 12:43.000
to have Celtic people singing and playing their music.

12:43.000 --> 12:47.000
Oh, and it became a little cottage industry,

12:47.000 --> 12:52.000
people would be taken down there to entertain the intelligentsia

12:52.000 --> 12:56.000
So, but when they came to write down the music,

12:56.000 --> 13:00.000
of course, they were restricted to what they had,

13:00.000 --> 13:04.000
they could not, they had no way of writing in the grace notes, 

13:04.000 --> 13:06.000
and that’s how Marjory Kennedy-Fraser, for one,

13:06.000 --> 13:10.000
made such a foolish collection of songs, 

13:10.000 --> 13:13.000
you take for example her version of the Eriskay Love Lilt

13:13.000 --> 13:19.000
which doesn’t even… it’s not even similar to the melody.

13:19.000 --> 13:22.000
Anyway, all these things put together 

13:22.000 --> 13:27.000
give you a picture of a people who were wise, 

13:27.000 --> 13:33.000
who had traveled far, because they had planted their song lines…

13:35.000 --> 13:39.000
all the way from the Himalayas, right through Southern Europe,

13:39.000 --> 13:43.000
the Middle East and on to Southern Europe right up to here. 

13:43.000 --> 13:46.000
Planting their song lines so that they could

13:46.000 --> 13:50.000
relay it physically to where they would be

13:50.000 --> 13:55.000
from one moment of their history to another.

13:55.000 --> 14:01.000
The nearest thing we have at the moment to them would be the Aborigines, 

14:01.000 --> 14:07.000
the Aborigines did a similar thing with their music and stories,

14:08.000 --> 14:15.000
and when, eventually, the tradition of the clan bard,

14:15.000 --> 14:19.000
like the MacMhuirichs of Stilligarry,

14:20.000 --> 14:25.000
and their main thing was not to write poetry, 

14:25.000 --> 14:28.000
apart from praising the clan chief,

14:28.000 --> 14:30.000
but to make the epic tales.

14:30.000 --> 14:33.000
And when I was a child, the epic tale,

14:33.000 --> 14:35.000
one of the MacMhuirich tales, would take

14:35.000 --> 14:38.000
perhaps a fortnight to be told.

14:38.000 --> 14:40.000
I would sit on an old man’s knee and he would tell,

14:40.000 --> 14:44.000
and that will be where we will leave them tonight,

14:44.000 --> 14:47.000
and you will now go to bed, and so it went on.

14:47.000 --> 14:50.000
Now that was not something they created

14:50.000 --> 14:52.000
in this part of the Celtic world,

14:52.000 --> 14:54.000
that was something they had taken with them

14:54.000 --> 14:57.000
all the way from the far East,

14:57.000 --> 14:59.000
the telling of the great epic tales.

14:59.000 --> 15:02.000
And if we found other things,

15:02.000 --> 15:10.000
and eventually intermixed it with the Viking world studies,

15:10.000 --> 15:13.000
we are to be excused because they were so similar.

15:13.000 --> 15:16.000
And that is why you will immediately find things like 

15:16.000 --> 15:20.000
the Valhalla, the Tìr nan Òg, the land of youth,

15:20.000 --> 15:23.000
the land below the sea. 

15:23.000 --> 15:28.000
The fact that the seal is the son or daughter of

15:28.000 --> 15:31.000
the King of Lochlann under a spell,

15:31.000 --> 15:37.000
all that, and the swan is the great, beautiful Viking lady

15:37.000 --> 15:38.000
and all these kind of things, 

15:38.000 --> 15:41.000
you can recognise immediately because

15:41.000 --> 15:45.000
it is all part and parcel of what we were about.

15:45.000 --> 15:50.000
Because, so as I say, from morning you woke up in the morning

15:50.000 --> 15:54.000
and the first thing you did was you celebrated 

15:54.000 --> 15:58.000
the fact that you were awake one more time.

15:59.000 --> 16:04.000
Because the rest of the symphony was that spring became summer

16:04.000 --> 16:08.000
became autumn became winter, and then everything died.

16:08.000 --> 16:10.000
Until the following spring. 

16:10.000 --> 16:14.000
But you kept on going because throughout the death part, 

16:14.000 --> 16:18.000
your songs were going on, your stories were going on,

16:18.000 --> 16:20.000
your living was going on and when you opened your eyes,

16:20.000 --> 16:22.000
that was the first thing that happened.

16:22.000 --> 16:27.000
So each day was a celebration of food and drink 

16:27.000 --> 16:31.000
and fellowship and laughter and song.

16:31.000 --> 16:35.000
That is the background of the people we belong to.

16:35.000 --> 16:37.000
So that coloured our lives and 

16:37.000 --> 16:42.000
colours our lives to this very day, to this very day,

16:42.000 --> 16:45.000
I mean, it would be a churlish person who would not, 

16:45.000 --> 16:47.000
while opening his eyes,

16:47.000 --> 16:52.000
celebrate and watch the tide coming and going,

16:52.000 --> 16:56.000
the wind blowing and the various things 

16:56.000 --> 16:59.000
that happened throughout the day.

17:00.000 --> 17:02.000
That was the sort of simple people we had,

17:02.000 --> 17:04.000
we never had to make a God, of course,

17:04.000 --> 17:07.000
I mean, you’ll find Jews and everybody else, 

17:07.000 --> 17:13.000
they were making their, Indians were making icons of various kinds

17:13.000 --> 17:18.000
and totem poles and things, and we never had to do that

17:18.000 --> 17:24.000
because what we worshiped was actually what was happening

17:24.000 --> 17:29.000
in the actual elements. So hey presto!

17:29.000 --> 17:31.000
When the Christian message came across,

17:31.000 --> 17:33.000
how did we deal with that?

17:33.000 --> 17:40.000
We looked at it and said: ‘yes… mm, yes, that’s right yes, mhm, mhm.

17:40.000 --> 17:45.000
Well, that’s exactly how, that’s the sort of God we could,

17:45.000 --> 17:49.000
we believe in all the time! Nothing new.’

17:51.000 --> 17:59.000
And so on, and: ‘ok fine, fine, we accept this,

17:59.000 --> 18:01.000
because it is what we are about.

18:01.000 --> 18:04.000
What we were always about. Nothing has changed.’

18:05.000 --> 18:09.000
But the simple conditions would be that you take with you

18:12.000 --> 18:16.000
the things that made you a special people.

18:17.000 --> 18:21.000
That’s why in giving the Christian message they, say,

18:21.000 --> 18:25.000
you take the Brits, the Brits were perfect at it.

18:25.000 --> 18:27.000
The first thing that did is they

18:27.000 --> 18:31.000
went to Africa or anywhere to convert people 

18:31.000 --> 18:33.000
to this new Christian thing,

18:33.000 --> 18:35.000
they first of all turn them into Brits.

18:35.000 --> 18:38.000
That’s the important thing – make them into Brits.

18:39.000 --> 18:45.000
And then, as Brits, make them accept and live the sort of life,

18:45.000 --> 18:50.000
leaving aside and forgetting what they were about themselves.

18:51.000 --> 18:55.000
That was a horrendous approach to it all. 

18:56.000 --> 19:01.000
We were sufficiently well-travelled and wise to know

19:01.000 --> 19:03.000
that we would hold on to whatever we wanted and

19:03.000 --> 19:07.000
whatever was precious in our heritage and our background,

19:07.000 --> 19:10.000
and so it was, that to this very day,

19:10.000 --> 19:15.000
you will find a Christian religion, by all means,

19:15.000 --> 19:18.000
but full of lovely little nuances 

19:18.000 --> 19:23.000
that puts the stamp of our kind of people on it.

19:24.000 --> 19:27.000
And that is so desperately important. 

19:27.000 --> 19:30.000
That is so desperately important, I mean,

19:30.000 --> 19:34.000
take any of the stories and we put flesh and blood on them.

19:35.000 --> 19:38.000
You look at the Christian Book for instance and it’s,

19:38.000 --> 19:42.000
the first thing you look for is the hero, the hero.

19:42.000 --> 19:46.000
Where do you find the hero in it? There is a hero.

19:48.000 --> 19:53.000
St Michael the Archangel is the one on the white steed,

19:53.000 --> 19:57.000
who absolutely threw the devil into the depths of hell,

19:57.000 --> 20:00.000
and the devil is everything that was unpleasant.

20:01.000 --> 20:03.000
Into the depths of hell, that’s the hero,

20:03.000 --> 20:06.000
so you start on the coast of France.

20:07.000 --> 20:12.000
Mont-Saint-Michel off the Breton coast. 

20:12.000 --> 20:18.000
You sail across to… Cornwall and there you’ve got

20:18.000 --> 20:20.000
Mount St Michael again, on the coast,

20:20.000 --> 20:25.000
and [Kilraine/?] on the west side of England, the west side of Ireland,

20:25.000 --> 20:29.000
the west side of Wales, and so wherever the Celtics

20:29.000 --> 20:34.000
planted their song lines, you go and look, and you will find 

20:35.000 --> 20:40.000
that Michael has always been traditional here, great man.

20:40.000 --> 20:46.000
We made him the Neptune of the show, in effect,

20:46.000 --> 20:50.000
he was responsible for two things: 

20:50.000 --> 20:57.000
the culmination of the harvest thing, 

20:57.000 --> 21:03.000
and the wealth brought in by the ocean to our shores.

21:04.000 --> 21:09.000
Through these centuries, Michael taught, Michael… put

21:09.000 --> 21:14.000
a stamp which no other Christian body, that’s why I’m giving him as an example,

21:14.000 --> 21:20.000
would ever recognise as being of any importance. 

21:21.000 --> 21:24.000
Michael, ok, was important everywhere

21:24.000 --> 21:26.000
but not of this kind of importance.

21:27.000 --> 21:35.000
Take Bride, similar, we give Bride all the…

21:36.000 --> 21:42.000
qualities of Dana, the goddess,

21:42.000 --> 21:44.000
and we put her in charge of the elements.

21:44.000 --> 21:46.000
She’s the one who put her elbow on the 1st of February,

21:46.000 --> 21:50.000
our first day of spring, 1st of February,

21:50.000 --> 21:52.000
she’s the one who put her elbow in and said

21:52.000 --> 21:54.000
‘the water is too cold’

21:54.000 --> 21:59.000
and she got the first pangs, the first life coming in

21:59.000 --> 22:02.000
on the 1st of February, into the soil.

22:03.000 --> 22:06.000
And we were traditionally, when, in South Uist, 

22:06.000 --> 22:10.000
when my father would gather with the family round the table

22:10.000 --> 22:17.000
in the little thatched cottage and he would recite the genealogy.

22:17.000 --> 22:21.000
Now that, again, goes right back to India.

22:22.000 --> 22:25.000
Who you are, I mean, ok your Americans are particularly good

22:25.000 --> 22:28.000
at looking for roots, you’re desperate to have roots

22:28.000 --> 22:33.000
because you have none since you left us, and so…

22:34.000 --> 22:37.000
what makes you, what gives you your dignity 

22:37.000 --> 22:39.000
and who you are is your roots.

22:39.000 --> 22:45.000
And your roots are based also in your genealogy. Nowhere else.

22:45.000 --> 22:50.000
You are Catriona, the daughter of Angus,

22:50.000 --> 22:53.000
the daughter of Patricia so on, you see,

22:53.000 --> 22:56.000
and before you know where you are I’ve got you

22:56.000 --> 22:58.000
on the other side of the Meditteranean.

22:58.000 --> 23:03.000
That is… that is exactly what this is about. 

23:03.000 --> 23:06.000
That the genealogy of Bride,

23:06.000 --> 23:12.000
by reciting her genealogy you are bringing, you are bringing alive again,

23:12.000 --> 23:14.000
by mentioning their names, you are bringing alive again

23:14.000 --> 23:18.000
all these, and you are invoking their aid

23:18.000 --> 23:20.000
as they come back to the surface, 

23:20.000 --> 23:24.000
through the water, through the hills, across the oceans,

23:24.000 --> 23:29.000
and you stand and my father would invoke them all.

23:30.000 --> 23:35.000
And then he would say: ‘Now that we have invoked your aid,

23:35.000 --> 23:39.000
Holy Bride, we will not be punished. 

23:39.000 --> 23:41.000
We will not suffer through the elements

23:41.000 --> 23:44.000
for the next twelve months and a day.

23:45.000 --> 23:48.000
We will not ever, ever, ever suffer.

23:48.000 --> 23:51.000
We will not be burned, we will not be set on fire,

23:51.000 --> 23:53.000
we will not be drowned we will not… 

23:53.000 --> 23:55.000
the elements will have no effect upon us

23:55.000 --> 23:59.000
because we are under the patronage and the mantle 

23:59.000 --> 24:01.000
and the cloak of Holy Bride.’

24:02.000 --> 24:06.000
You see how what we’ve done in adopting,

24:08.000 --> 24:13.000
accepting Christianity, but putting our little, local stamp on it.

24:13.000 --> 24:16.000
So the next important thing that happened of course

24:16.000 --> 24:24.000
was the Reformation, and when… when everything was over and 

24:24.000 --> 24:30.000
the dust settled a bit, then the new Protestant thing relayed:

24:30.000 --> 24:33.000
‘Oh God, we must get rid of all this terrible superstition

24:33.000 --> 24:38.000
and put it under and hide it, get rid of it and just have the Good Book.’

24:39.000 --> 24:44.000
Just have the Good Book, just that, which denuded

24:44.000 --> 24:45.000
– or at least they attempted –

24:45.000 --> 24:49.000
to denude and take away the flesh and…

24:49.000 --> 24:51.000
flesh off the bones and we were left with the bones

24:51.000 --> 24:56.000
of something which was relevant only, to a large extent,

24:56.000 --> 24:58.000
to a strange Jewish people

24:58.000 --> 25:03.000
being dragged all the way from… from Egypt,

25:03.000 --> 25:05.000
through long deserts and so on.

25:05.000 --> 25:09.000
And very little of it had any sort of relevance,

25:09.000 --> 25:11.000
But you see, what gave them relevance and 

25:11.000 --> 25:19.000
the meaning of all this is who we are and what we are about.

25:20.000 --> 25:25.000
So are you getting the picture now of what we’ve done? 

25:25.000 --> 25:29.000
Um, towards the end of the eighteenth century 

25:29.000 --> 25:32.000
into the nineteenth century, there was 

25:32.000 --> 25:40.000
a big revival and all kinds of people were influential like [XX] and so on,

25:40.000 --> 25:42.000
encouraged all kinds of people. 

25:42.000 --> 25:47.000
And eventually we had Carmichael’s Carmina Gadelica,

25:47.000 --> 25:50.000
which he wrote in conjunction with many other friends

25:50.000 --> 25:52.000
like Ailean MacDonald.

25:52.000 --> 26:03.000
And reviving and restoring the old rùns, incantations and so on.

26:03.000 --> 26:06.000
Oh, that was lovely eh, that came back.

26:06.000 --> 26:12.000
When, in my youth, in a Catholic atmosphere

26:12.000 --> 26:15.000
we kept all this alive, of course.

26:16.000 --> 26:19.000
I remember one of my earliest recollections is,

26:19.000 --> 26:22.000
on this particular day I heard a horrendous cry going up

26:22.000 --> 26:24.000
and the old lady who was two crofts along,

26:24.000 --> 26:27.000
she was weeping and I said to my father,

26:27.000 --> 26:31.000
I said: ‘come out quickly, Mòr is weeping.’

26:31.000 --> 26:34.000
And he went out and he shouted to her,

26:34.000 --> 26:39.000
and she said the cow was dead… was dead or dying, 

26:39.000 --> 26:41.000
that it had been cursed and that someone

26:41.000 --> 26:43.000
had put the evil eye on it.

26:43.000 --> 26:47.000
And I remember distinctly going to a lady

26:47.000 --> 26:50.000
in one of the villages to have the threads made,

26:50.000 --> 26:53.000
the red threads which went on the thingmy. 

26:53.000 --> 26:56.000
I remember all that was still in existence

26:56.000 --> 26:59.000
at the beginning of this present century, which is now dying.

27:00.000 --> 27:05.000
All that in existence before, until radio, television,

27:05.000 --> 27:09.000
electricity and things came, we were a people without books, 

27:09.000 --> 27:15.000
we were a people of an oral tradition in which we, wasn’t…

27:16.000 --> 27:21.000
composed our history, uncovered our history,

27:21.000 --> 27:23.000
and a colourful history it was.

27:23.000 --> 27:27.000
Embellished, at times, for the telling of the tale, you see, 

27:27.000 --> 27:30.000
the important thing for the Aborigene,

27:30.000 --> 27:34.000
the important thing for the Celt, is that the bàrd,

27:34.000 --> 27:37.000
I’m of a bardic strain, the village bardic strain,

27:37.000 --> 27:41.000
that the bard knows how to tell the story, 

27:41.000 --> 27:43.000
that is terribly important.

27:43.000 --> 27:45.000
And if you look at St Paul, 

27:45.000 --> 27:48.000
St Paul was excellent at telling the story.

27:48.000 --> 27:51.000
If you look at St Paul, and he could tell the story and

27:51.000 --> 27:54.000
it just put on, again,

27:54.000 --> 28:00.000
put flesh on the bones to make it a story worth the telling.

28:00.000 --> 28:03.000
Some of the evangelists, some of them were very pragmatic, 

28:03.000 --> 28:07.000
but others could just again do that little bit

28:07.000 --> 28:10.000
of putting colour on it.

28:11.000 --> 28:16.000
And so, all these things show the kind of people we are

28:16.000 --> 28:18.000
and if we are a strange people, 

28:18.000 --> 28:19.000
and of course we are a strange people,

28:19.000 --> 28:24.000
because we are a people who have fought hard to preserve our identity 

28:24.000 --> 28:27.000
and when you consider this a minority culture,

28:27.000 --> 28:31.000
a minority language, that we had to wait until Europe

28:31.000 --> 28:36.000
became a broad country before they would even listen to us

28:36.000 --> 28:40.000
as being, as having any kind of importance as a people.

28:40.000 --> 28:44.000
We have survived through it all.

28:44.000 --> 28:46.000
We have survived marvelously well.

28:48.000 --> 28:52.000
When I was a boy, it was forbidden by law 

28:53.000 --> 28:58.000
to teach our language in the school.

28:59.000 --> 29:02.000
I had never heard the English spoken until I was five

29:02.000 --> 29:04.000
and the first day I went to school, the teacher was 

29:04.000 --> 29:07.000
speaking to me in a language I did not understand,

29:07.000 --> 29:10.000
and I had to learn the English 

29:10.000 --> 29:14.000
in order to add one and one and make it two.

29:15.000 --> 29:18.000
It was only much later that the law relented

29:18.000 --> 29:22.000
and allowed us to have our own language.

29:22.000 --> 29:27.000
And by the early 30s, now…

29:29.000 --> 29:35.000
it had got a bit of competition and there were stalwarts like… 

29:38.000 --> 29:44.000
[XX] and others who started running camps

29:44.000 --> 29:50.000
for Gaelic-speaking children from the various islands together in summer,

29:50.000 --> 29:52.000
summer camps and things.

29:52.000 --> 29:57.000
And it was only from there that things started moving very, very slowly 

29:57.000 --> 30:02.000
and few of the universities would have a Celtic chair.

30:04.000 --> 30:10.000
And to this day, I mean, it’s scandalous that there are only Celtic chairs,

30:10.000 --> 30:12.000
there should be, there is not a single,

30:12.000 --> 30:16.000
not one of the universities has the courage to have a Gaelic chair. 

30:18.000 --> 30:23.000
I bring this out in a new preface to one of our books,

30:23.000 --> 30:28.000
historical books, which is being republished after a hundred years.

30:30.000 --> 30:36.000
So from all that blurb, whatever happens should not surprise you

30:36.000 --> 30:38.000
in how people tell their tales. 

30:38.000 --> 30:42.000
I mean, I was brought up on the MacMhuirich tales and uh…

30:44.000 --> 30:45.000
how the people tell the tales,

30:45.000 --> 30:49.000
the important thing is that they do know how to tell the tales.

30:49.000 --> 30:55.000
I have, in a religious broadcast just a few months ago, 

30:55.000 --> 31:00.000
I explained how my very first memory is all the dolphins,

31:00.000 --> 31:02.000
whichever they were, offshore in the west,

31:02.000 --> 31:04.000
when suddenly I saw the line.

31:04.000 --> 31:08.000
For the first time in my life, I said to him: ‘what is that line?’

31:08.000 --> 31:12.000
and he said ‘that is the line of the horizon.’    

31:12.000 --> 31:17.000
And I said: ‘Oh, what happens over that line?’

31:17.000 --> 31:21.000
‘Oh, I’m going there very soon,’ he said.

31:23.000 --> 31:27.000
‘Why?’ I said. ‘Because over that line,’ he says,

31:27.000 --> 31:31.000
‘is the place of real and eternal youth, 

31:31.000 --> 31:35.000
where everybody is happy for ever after,

31:35.000 --> 31:37.000
and I’m going there soon.’

31:37.000 --> 31:42.000
I said, I got a hold of him, I remember very clearly,

31:42.000 --> 31:44.000
I got a hold of him and said: ‘Good, I’ll go with you.’

31:44.000 --> 31:47.000
And he said: ‘Yes, you’ll come sometime but not now. 

31:47.000 --> 31:49.000
You’re too young, it’s only old people like me

31:49.000 --> 31:52.000
who will go, but one day you will come.’

31:52.000 --> 31:54.000
And I said: ‘Will you be waiting for me?’

31:54.000 --> 31:59.000
He said: ‘Yes I will! And, oh, we will be so happy together.’

31:59.000 --> 32:02.000
And he described that. Now, 

32:02.000 --> 32:08.000
that was a simple way of telling it, so I [XX],

32:09.000 --> 32:18.000
as soon, as early as I was conscious of getting a Christian story

32:18.000 --> 32:22.000
of any sort, and it was and to this day 

32:22.000 --> 32:28.000
as important, in my book, as any Christian story.

32:28.000 --> 32:36.000
Because, would I… most clergy of any Christian denomination

32:36.000 --> 32:41.000
would tell you that we should, we should wipe out all these things

32:41.000 --> 32:44.000
and just look at the New Testament and the Old Testament,

32:44.000 --> 32:47.000
and ok, you do that, 

32:47.000 --> 32:52.000
but you’re very colourless, dull people. 

32:52.000 --> 32:58.000
I much prefer to live a life of 

32:58.000 --> 33:02.000
great celebration and joy with the elements.

33:02.000 --> 33:06.000
That’s my kind of life because it’s the life that I’m used to

33:06.000 --> 33:07.000
and I, in my little way, 

33:07.000 --> 33:11.000
am planting my song lines,

33:11.000 --> 33:16.000
so that our people yet to come will stand on a little rock,

33:16.000 --> 33:20.000
little tuft of [XX] and say ‘ah’.

33:20.000 --> 33:25.000
The birds there and this is where it happened. 

33:25.000 --> 33:29.000
Voila! You got anything?

33:29.000 --> 33:30.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

33:30.000 --> 33:33.000
…having made the scones for the tea

33:33.000 --> 33:37.000
and there would be an argument and a song and a composition 

33:37.000 --> 33:39.000
about the day’s happenings,

33:40.000 --> 33:46.000
and we incorporated the new history and I mean,

33:46.000 --> 33:49.000
I remember they were talking about fighting in the Boer War,

33:49.000 --> 33:52.000
the First World War of course and when we played games

33:52.000 --> 33:56.000
we played pontoon for matches,

33:56.000 --> 33:59.000
because when you were in the trenches,

33:59.000 --> 34:01.000
the last thing you’d better light was a match,

34:01.000 --> 34:05.000
’cause it would mean a sniper would get you and… 

34:09.000 --> 34:10.000
so there you are.